    "media:imagetext": "PAAN\nPAN AMERICAN AIRWAYS\nCOMPANY\nGENERAL OFFICE\nFirsonal and\nALAMEDA\n- CALIFORNIA\nConfidentice\nOctober 7, 1937\nDISTRICT TRAFFIC OFFICE\nMANILA HOTEL\nMANILA, P. I.\nDear Mr. Morgan\nand\nI have just returned from Hongkong, am taking this\nonly opportunity to rush off a short letter to you via Clipper\nleaving tonight in order to give you some of the background\nwhich\nmay assist you in understanding cabled recommendations which otherwise\nmight be wholly non-understandable.\nWe have been diligently searching for some method\nwhich offers some hope of salvage for PAA in our Chinese effort. Such\nan operation must have the advantage of not weakening our present sit-\nuation respecting possible claims against the Government of China (if\nthere be any such entity when the war is over) and yet represent what the\nChinese may call a constructive program.\nIn Hongkong I met Bond, Lum and P.I. Wong.\nThe Managing\nDirector ( Lum) has clearly been\n• told to get our cooperation for he fairly\ncrawled and presented a truly pathetic spectacle in face of his former\nswash buckling attitude, He said quite\nback\".\nin terrible condition,\npublic is losing confidence.\nOne Chinese pilot\n, their best one Donald\nWong) has lost so much confidence and was so badly treated by his own\npeople that he went AWOL and is now on his way to the U,S. Other Chinese\npilots are getting fedup with the Chinese management,\nThe Company needs\n• new motors\nand most of\ndie Prestige rech went eagedated) of a Connection with American\nall they need\noperating personnel. They are ready tomake any reasonable compromise to\nsecure our cooperation.\nUnder the circumstances Bond and I feel that we may be\nable to get a definite \"fix\" on the liability of the Government to the American\nshareholders and at the same time set the stage for a come back in the\nevent that the Chinese should come out of the present struggle with at\nleast a part of their country left andready for reconstruction.\nAny discussion\nof renewing our participation should be subject to the following conditions\n1. Agreement by the Chinese Govt that the Company will be run on a strictly\ncommercial basis and that the moment it is not the whole American personnel\nwill be withdrawn.\n2. Recognition of the commereial status of CNAC as a\njoint commercial venture by the U.S. govt or if that is not possible then\nimplied recognition through notice from us to the U.Sthat we are going to resun\nactive association\nwith the Chinese in the operation of the Company with noti\nmatic also to the Japanese that such operation will be take n up and that we are\nengaged in the operation of civil aircraft, away from the fighting zones\ncarrying mail, passengers and non- military express. (We should paint all\nour airplanes the Post Office green and clearly mark them. 3. An agree-\nment on the part of the Govt to extend the present contract 15 years. 4. Pay-\nment by CNAC of the balance of its indebtedness to PAA including $ 25,000 U.S.\nfor the Dolphin. 5. CNAC to agree to take over Route 3 at a figure to\nbe named\nnow and not subject to any trading whatever, this figure to be considerably\nunder the actual amount invested by PAA.\nIne sum of $400,000 U.S. is suggested\n* No injures.\nPAN\nSpartan\nOne Dc - 2\nAIR W\nwhuch\n5 - 1 3 5\nwar\n2 Fords\nSENERAL.\ngurn to\nI Loning! St\nStinson\nend the\nCNAC\nfor\nsaving it:\"",
